|term-group[ddd:anticorruption-layer][ddd:open-host-service][ddd:published-language]
  :status  proposed
  :source  "Eric Evans, Domain-Driven Design Reference: Definitions and Pattern Summaries (Domain Language, 2015), CC BY 4.0 — local copy ref/DDD_Reference_2015-03/"

  When two @{ddd:bounded-context}s must communicate and neither will conform to the other, someone translates. Evans gives three shapes for where the translation lives. An **ANTICORRUPTION-LAYER** is built by the downstream for itself: it takes the upstream as it is and renders it in the downstream's own model, so the downstream model is not bent toward the upstream's. An **OPEN-HOST-SERVICE** is built by the upstream for everyone: one open protocol that any client can integrate with. A **PUBLISHED-LANGUAGE** is a documented common medium that each side translates into and out of. Evans advises against making either side's own model that medium. The three combine. An open host often speaks a published language, and its clients either conform to it or build anticorruption layers against it.

  |term[ddd:anticorruption-layer] An isolating layer, built by a downstream context, that provides it with the upstream's functionality in terms of its own model, translating between the two models.
    :source "[SOURCE: Evans 2015, Anticorruption Layer, p. 34, modified — 'As a downstream client, create an isolating layer to provide your system with functionality of the upstream system in terms of your own domain model … Internally, the layer translates in one or both directions as necessary between the two models.' restated as a definition]"
    :avoid  [adapter] ; one implementation technique, not the pattern
    |rels :built-by   @{ddd:bounded-context} {1,1}  ; the downstream
          :isolates-from @{ddd:bounded-context} {1,1}  ; the upstream
    |conditions
      - Cooperation is not available: "when control or communication is not adequate to pull off a shared kernel, partner or customer/supplier relationship, translation becomes more complex. The translation layer takes on a more defensive tone." (p. 34)
      - The upstream's model does not fit: "The models of legacy systems are usually weak (if not big balls of mud), and even the exception that is clearly designed may not fit the needs of the current project, making it impractical to conform to the upstream model." (p. 34)
    |invariants
      - It belongs to the downstream: "As a downstream client, create an isolating layer" (p. 34).
      - It leaves the upstream as it is: it "talks to the other system through its existing interface, requiring little or no modification to the other system" (p. 34).
      - It translates in whichever directions are needed: "the layer translates in one or both directions as necessary between the two models" (p. 34).
      - What it protects against is the downstream model drifting toward the upstream's: "A large interface with an upstream system can eventually overwhelm the intent of the downstream model altogether, causing it to be modified to resemble the other system's model in an ad hoc fashion." (p. 34)

  |term[ddd:open-host-service] A protocol, opened by a context to all who need to integrate with it, that gives access to that context as a set of services.
    :source "[SOURCE: Evans 2015, Open-host Service, p. 35, modified — 'Define a protocol that gives access to your subsystem as a set of services. Open the protocol so that all who need to integrate with you can use it.' restated as a definition]"
    |conditions
      - "When a subsystem has to be integrated with many others, customizing a translator for each can bog down the team." (p. 35)
    |invariants
      - Its provider is upstream of every client: "This places the provider of the service in the upstream position. Each client is downstream, and typically some of them will be conformist and some will build anticorruption layers." (p. 35)
      - It is one protocol for all, not one per client. Idiosyncratic needs get a one-off translator outside it, "so that the shared protocol can stay simple and coherent" (p. 35).
    |practices
      - "Enhance and expand the protocol to handle new integration requirements, except when a single team has idiosyncratic needs." (p. 35)

  |term[ddd:published-language] A well-documented shared language that can express the necessary domain information as a common medium of communication, into and out of which each context translates.
    :source "[SOURCE: Evans 2015, Published Language, p. 36, modified — 'Use a well-documented shared language that can express the necessary domain information as a common medium of communication, translating as necessary into and out of that language.' restated as a definition]"
    |conditions
      - "The translation between the models of two bounded contexts requires a common language." (p. 36)
    |invariants
      - It is documented and shared: "a well-documented shared language" (p. 36).
      - It is a medium of translation: each context translates "as necessary into and out of that language" (p. 36).
    |practices
      - Don't make either side's own model the medium. Evans's advice is hedged: "Direct translation to and from the existing domain models may not be a good solution." His reasons: "Those models may be overly complex or poorly factored. They are probably undocumented. If one is used as a data interchange language, it essentially becomes frozen and cannot respond to new development needs." (p. 36)
    |examples
      - "Many industries establish published languages in the form of data interchange standards. Project teams also develop their own for use within their organization." (p. 36)
      - "Published language is often combined with open-host service." (p. 36)

  |discussion
    - **This project's translations are an anticorruption layer.** Each source is upstream and this project is downstream. The sources are read through their "existing interface", their published text, and never modified, which matches "little or no modification to the other system" and the project's compilation principle. The danger it guards against is a downstream model "modified to resemble the other system's model in an ad hoc fashion". Model beta's adoption of IASR 2026's definitions was a different and deliberate move (conformist, plan §1.2), though it ended in the same place. The per-source translations are the inside of the layer (plan §3.2). Two widenings, ours: Evans's layer provides an upstream's *functionality*, and ours provides a source's *claims and meanings*; and ours translates in one direction only, from source to us. — our reading
    - **This project's lexicon is not a published language.** It is this project's own ubiquitous language: a model with commitments, not a neutral medium (plan §2: "Neutrality lives in the assertions, not the vocabulary"). Evans's warning applies if it is ever offered as one: a domain model used for interchange "essentially becomes frozen". If others are to integrate with this project's records, a separate published language would be the cleaner route. — our reading
    - **Candidates in the corpus**, none checked for this entry. A statute's definitions read like an open-host service, since other instruments integrate with them and some conform (the EU Code to the AI Act). Shared vocabularies built for interchange read like published languages: MIT's AI Risk Repository taxonomies, ISO/IEC 22989's terminology, and NIST's "AI actors" (adopted from the OECD). Whether any of them meets Evans's conditions (documented, shared, nobody's own model) is open. — our reading

  |examples
    |   Situation                                                                 |  Term                               |
    |---------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|
    | This project's translation of SB 53 into its lexicon                      | part of an @{ddd:anticorruption-layer} |
    | The AI Act's definitions, which the EU Code declares will prevail          | candidate @{ddd:open-host-service}, with the Code @{ddd:conformist} to it |
    | A data-interchange standard for incident reports, if one existed           | a @{ddd:published-language}           |

  |working-notes
    - Open: whether a translation that adds attributed readings ("our reading") is still purely a layer, or partly this project's model leaking into the layer. The plan keeps the source's words and our reading side by side, which keeps the two apart.
    - Open: an anticorruption layer usually also protects against the upstream's *changes*. Here that is the as-of index on every resolution, and the re-resolution a source revision triggers (address theory's dangle, plan §3.3). No entry yet joins the two vocabularies.
